AT-6 under SEC-3 want mode — stock Grafana with NO client certificate
2026-08-10 · timelakedb-tls + grafana/grafana:latest, client CA configured
================================================================================

THE QUESTION: does asking every client for a certificate break the
clients that do not have one? For this project that is not academic —
FR-8/FR-9 make stock Grafana and Telegraf first-class, and a required-
mTLS server would fail every panel below.

  python bench/compose/tls-drill/at6_grafana_mtls.py
  11/11 checks passed

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY DRIVEN
  Grafana's OWN Flight SQL datasource, through /api/ds/query — not a
  Python client imitating it. That is the same code path a rendering
  browser takes, so a plugin-level TLS incompatibility could not hide.

  datasource: https://timelakedb-tls:1964, insecureGrpc=false,
              CA pinned, secureJsonFields = []  <- no client cert, none
  server:     timelake_tls_client_auth_mode 1   <- asking for one anyway

  ALL 58 PANEL QUERIES from the four fixture dashboards executed and
  returned data. 58/58 non-empty. Zero failures.
    host_metrics (fleet), host_detail (single host, $host bound),
    pipeline_funnel (24 h funnel, DISTINCT product counts),
    product_journey ($product_id bound)

BOTH PATHS, SAME LISTENER, SAME RUN
  A pyarrow Flight SQL client presenting client-tributary-node-1.crt
  connected and got its rows back, in the same container run in which
  Grafana connected with nothing. The server told them apart:

    timelake_flight_connections_authenticated_total 1
    timelake_flight_connections_anonymous_total     58

  THOSE TWO COUNTERS ARE THE POINT, and they did not exist before this
  drill was written. Want mode is otherwise invisible: both paths return
  200, so nothing tells an operator whether anybody is presenting
  certificates yet. Flipping a listener to require them without knowing
  that ratio is how an outage happens. 58-to-1 says this deployment has
  not migrated, and the drill asserts exactly that.

  (The identity also appears in the log — "flight client authenticated
  peer=... identity=tributary-node-1" — but that line is debug-level,
  so asserting on it would measure RUST_LOG rather than behaviour. The
  drill asserts on the counters instead.)

AT-7 NON-REGRESSION: still 19/19 after the accept loop changed
  Threading the counters through the accept loop touched the same
  `unfold` that carries the TLS acceptor, so rotation was re-drilled:
  both listeners rotate in lockstep, a 19.1 s in-flight Flight SQL query
  completes across the swap with an exact result, 132 writes acked and
  132 queryable, Telegraf over TLS clean, corrupt renewal rejected with
  SEC3_CERT_RENEWAL_FAILED while the last-good pair keeps serving.

RIG
  bench/compose/timelakedb-tls.yml gained a `grafana` profile whose
  datasource deliberately configures NO client certificate — if that
  service ever needs one to work, want mode has regressed into required
  mode and this drill fails.

  docker compose -f compose/timelakedb-tls.yml --profile grafana up -d
